+# EXEC0145 — first-Spring chronology, observed original stock fresh reset
+
+Inherit complete EXEC0144 native C11 source. This addendum adds `gk145_fresh_progress.c/.h`, linked into `gk1_app_state`, with hostile validation of sequential frame/cycle, 14/27-country onset, year, Rate, Gold and original three-order transition. `test_gk145_fresh_progress` includes synthetic positive/negative assertions and the linked native app refusal before source templates are loaded. The analysis is *read only* and cannot fabricate first-Spring initialization, RNG seeds, a source-defined setup call sequence, any of the three original playable actions, AI or future seasons.
+
+A separate private executable test on the 14 exact authenticated original fresh-reset Mesen screenshots/event JSON produced the milestones: country count 14 first present at capture 2, Spring 1175 and Rate121 at capture 10, Gold425->430 first observed at capture 12, and original three orders first at capture 14. Source original NES SHA256 `ada7066d9e71a81310fff78bc7037126798018a69dc2b92382c3cbdcd9b243b3`; evidence ZIP SHA256 `8b757e11b12aeb3efe93e36c10628599ca5f12e91b15c02354cd3ebbd11bf52a`. Different RNG histories have different gold and rate: DO NOT hardcode these from this particular run. No new Mesen run or independent original first-Spring generation. No private raw event JSON, original ROM, savestate, screenshots/audio included in public code.
+
+Reproduction: `cmake -S execution_145 -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && cmake --build build -j8 && ctest --test-dir build -j8 --output-on-failure`. The only verified positive original timeline is Mongol fresh reset. Archived Mongol Spring 1175 and World 1206 are separate restored states and are never treated as fresh chronology or universal values. Native Linux tested; Win32/NSIS/physical Xbox/full campaigns not run. Future implementer must trace source-authentic setup/difficulty/ability RNG and PPU chronology, then generate independent Spring within same app, not turn this observation into playable state.
